NXR-211|Exclusive durable board lease|Host executive|The host manager shall lease a target by durable board identity and reject a concurrent owner even if the serial port changes.|A COM port name is neither a durable target identity nor a sufficient ownership lock.|Swap enumeration, run a second manager and hotplug the board; identity remains stable and exclusivity holds.|Use fake serial endpoints plus two real processes contending for one configured board.|NXR-076,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|One bounded session per configured board; OS lease lifetime tied to owner process.|docs/hil-verification-plan.rst,requirements/hil-design.csv|A COM port name is neither a durable target identity nor a sufficient ownership lock.
NXR-212|Independent session supervision|Host executive|A blocked target shall not block progress collection, deadlines or cleanup for other targets.|A shared blocking serial loop would turn one board failure into a bench-wide loss of evidence.|Stall one reader and disconnect another while a third completes its declared run without missed deadlines.|Execute a three-endpoint scenario with bounded asynchronous cancellation and retained per-board logs.|NXR-076,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Finite per-board queues and deadlines; no unbounded task creation.|docs/hil-verification-plan.rst,requirements/hil-design.csv|A shared blocking serial loop would turn one board failure into a bench-wide loss of evidence.
NXR-213|Immutable start manifest|Host executive|Before START the host shall freeze requested and effective firmware, board, checker, schema and workload identities.|Desired settings are not evidence that the target actually accepted those settings.|Firmware hash substitution or mismatched capability rejects START; later configuration edits create a new run identity.|Tamper with each manifest component between configuration and arming.|NXR-076,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|One immutable manifest per run; checked file sizes and hashes.|docs/hil-verification-plan.rst,requirements/hil-design.csv|Desired settings are not evidence that the target actually accepted those settings.
NXR-214|Bounded evidence writing|Host executive|Evidence backpressure shall terminate or explicitly degrade the run without silently losing mandatory terminal records.|Durable evidence failures must be distinguished from a healthy target pass.|Fill the host storage quota and stall the writer; result becomes incomplete and first-fault retention remains accounted for.|Use a quota-limited sink and injected I/O failures with a healthy control run.|NXR-076,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Declared bytes-per-run ceiling and bounded writer queue; no unlimited RAM buffering.|docs/hil-verification-plan.rst,requirements/hil-design.csv|Durable evidence failures must be distinguished from a healthy target pass.
NXR-215|Per-board terminal classification|Host executive|The host shall classify target failure, link loss, unresponsive target, stop timeout, host cutoff and incomplete evidence separately.|Transport silence cannot reveal whether the kernel, cable or host reader failed.|Each injected failure maps to exactly its documented classification; timeout alone cannot assert kernel failure.|Drive fake-device state transitions and physical unplug/reset cases.|NXR-076,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|One terminal result per run with append-only transition history.|docs/hil-verification-plan.rst,requirements/hil-design.csv|Transport silence cannot reveal whether the kernel, cable or host reader failed.
NXR-216|Host restart recovery|Host executive|After manager restart the host shall reconcile archived identity and live target state before issuing any state-changing command.|Process restart is a separate state transition from target reset.|A live old run is attached as observed or stopped by explicit policy; it is never silently duplicated.|Kill the host after START and before terminal persistence, then restart with persisted manifest.|NXR-076,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Bounded recovery handshake deadline and one active lease.|docs/hil-verification-plan.rst,requirements/hil-design.csv|Process restart is a separate state transition from target reset.
NXR-217|Cancellation cleanup evidence|Host executive|Host cancellation shall record stop request, acknowledgement, cleanup deadline and residual target state.|Returning from a cancelled task does not prove that the device stopped running.|Cancel during handshake, running and result fetch; each phase preserves a truthful terminal outcome.|Use cancellation injection at every awaited operation and a target that refuses stop.|NXR-076,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Finite cleanup allowance; no indefinite wait after host cutoff.|docs/hil-verification-plan.rst,requirements/hil-design.csv|Returning from a cancelled task does not prove that the device stopped running.
NXR-218|Raw and decoded evidence linkage|Host executive|Every decoded report shall retain a link to raw bytes and decoder version for later reanalysis.|JSON summaries alone cannot diagnose decoder defects or framing loss.|A malformed frame remains available as raw evidence; changing decoder version produces a separate interpretation.|Replay an archived byte stream through two schema versions and compare stable raw hashes.|NXR-076,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Raw capture quota declared; any discarded bytes have explicit counters.|docs/hil-verification-plan.rst,requirements/hil-design.csv|JSON summaries alone cannot diagnose decoder defects or framing loss.
NXR-219|Safe test selection|Host executive|The manager shall start only declared allowlisted test IDs and reject arbitrary address, register or memory-write requests.|The HIL monitor is a test executive rather than an unrestricted remote debugger.|Unknown IDs, out-of-range parameters and unsupported fault injection fail before START.|Fuzz command selection and validate that no raw debug command reaches the monitor.|NXR-076,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Finite test registry and bounded parameter schema.|docs/hil-verification-plan.rst,requirements/hil-design.csv|The HIL monitor is a test executive rather than an unrestricted remote debugger.
NXR-220|Host codec performance budget|Host executive|The host shall measure throughput and allocation behavior under the declared maximum board count without starving supervision.|Async code alone does not prove bounded resource use at bench scale.|At configured board count and report rate all deadlines remain serviced; overload reports queue pressure explicitly.|Replay maximal frames from simulated endpoints and sample memory, CPU and per-board deadline delay.|NXR-076,NXR-094|Opt-in qualification profile; boot-static storage; single CPU unless explicitly qualified otherwise.|Maximum board count, queue depth and host memory ceiling fixed before qualification.|docs/hil-verification-plan.rst,requirements/hil-design.csv|Async code alone does not prove bounded resource use at bench scale.
